## 1. Production-Ready Turbopack with Persistent Dependency Graphs

Turbopack reaches full stability across development environments (`next dev --turbo`) and production build targets (`next build`), officially retiring legacy Webpack configurations. This architectural leap is powered by an evaluation engine written in Rust that models dependency graphs at the atomic function boundary rather than across entire monolithic module trees.

| Performance Metric | Legacy Webpack | Turbopack (Next.js 16) | Improvement Delta |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Server Cold Start** | ~4.2 seconds | ~1.1 seconds | **~73% faster** |
| **Fast Refresh (HMR)** | ~850 ms | ~45 ms | **~94% faster** |
| **Build RAM Footprint** | ~580 MB | < 210 MB | **~64% reduction** |
| **Idle Standalone RAM** | ~240 MB | < 90 MB | **~62% reduction** |

```bash
# Production build with native Turbopack engine
next build --experimental-turbo
```

