## 4. Memory Footprint Optimization for 1 GB RAM VPS Instances

In budget cloud deployments (AWS Lightsail running Debian 13), unconstrained Node.js runtimes frequently experience memory spikes exceeding 350 MB. Next.js 16's `output: 'standalone'` flag outputs a minimal bundle excluding unused `devDependencies`, locking baseline idle memory consumption to **under 90 MB**.

> [!TIP]
> To guarantee that the V8 Garbage Collector reclaims memory before reaching operating system limits, always declare `--max-old-space-size=250` inside your PM2 ecosystem definition (`pm2.config.js`). This triggers heap compaction before the Linux kernel OOM Killer intervenes.

```javascript
// pm2.config.js - Production memory containment configuration
module.exports = {
  apps: [
    {
      name: 'frontend-next',
      script: './server.js',
      cwd: './frontend/web/.next/standalone/frontend/web',
      node_args: '--max-old-space-size=250',
      max_memory_restart: '220M',
      env: {
        NODE_ENV: 'production',
        PORT: 3001,
        HOSTNAME: 'localhost'
      }
    }
  ]
};
```

---

## 5. Architectural Conclusion and Takeaway

Next.js 16 is far more than an incremental speed bump; it fundamentally alters infrastructure economics for multi-tenant deployments. The convergence of Rust compilation, asynchronous React 19 primitives, and standalone packaging allows senior teams to deploy resilient, high-throughput systems on exceptionally lean hardware footprints.
